<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for the macOS .app.

  Exists for exactly one reason: cargo-packager signs every Mach-O in the bundle
  with `--options runtime`, unconditionally, with no configuration to turn that
  off. The hardened runtime enforces library validation, which allows a process
  to load only libraries signed by the same Team ID or by Apple. An ad-hoc
  signature carries no Team ID, so the ad-hoc signed binary was refused its own
  ad-hoc signed libMoltenVK.dylib:

      code signature ... not valid for use in process:
      mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have different Team IDs

  MoltenVK is the whole graphics stack on macOS, so 0.1.14 shipped an app that
  could not render at all. Disabling library validation restores the behaviour
  the bundle had before it was signed this way, and is the correct entitlement
  for an app that dlopens a library it ships itself.

  This is not a workaround for a missing Developer ID: a notarized build loading
  a third-party dylib it bundles needs the same entitlement.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
